{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(1-t)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1}=(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(t-1)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-\\frac{t-1}{t} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=\\frac{1-t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-t}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},g(t)=t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce930bc3b51acc39eca6c9d9c3f52897a765d94a064dfd3416e57ebae7a9603"} {"question" "q-337105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}+1"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-2t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{2}+t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, let $u=\\frac{t}{3}.$ Then $t=3u$ and $\\text{d}t=3\\,\\text{d}u,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (3u){e}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\int u{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9(u-1){e}^{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back $u=\\frac{t}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =9(\\frac{t}{3}-1){e}^{t/3} \\\\  & =(3t-9){e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(3t-9){e}^{t/3}+C \\\\  & =(-4t+12){e}^{t/3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx=-4t+12+C{e}^{-t/3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+12-4t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=12-4t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce930bc3b51acc39eca6c9d9c3f52897a765d94a064dfd3416e57ebae7a9603"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(t)=12+4t"] ["math" "f(t)=12-2t"] ["math" "f(t)=12-4t"] ["math" "f(t)=4-12t"] ["math" "f(t)=9-4t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(t)=12-4t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3)+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t=(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3}) & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}+C \\\\ x & =-4t-12+C{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t-3)+2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)(t-3)+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t-3,\\text{ }g(t)=2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce930bc3b51acc39eca6c9d9c3f52897a765d94a064dfd3416e57ebae7a9603"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{t}^{2}+t-3"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}+t+3"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}+t-3"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}+t-6"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}-t-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-337850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{t}^{2}+t-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-365372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-365372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+3 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-365372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ce930bc3b51acc39eca6c9d9c3f52897a765d94a064dfd3416e57ebae7a9603"} {"question" "q-365372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-