eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-365378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062485/state.json" "file_sha256" "bae3fd3b2eb3f0a58edff3572d701c75abf13ff37a76963516ced5d02b556dec"} {"question" "q-365378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ -\\frac{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/q